 ## Background
-The need for distributed training in 3D Gaussian Splatting arises from the quadratic growth of memory requirements with scene complexity and resolution. Early implementations were limited to single-GPU setups, restricting the scale of reconstructible scenes. The development of distributed methods has been driven by two main applications: large-scale urban reconstruction (e.g., BlitzGS, Grendel) and scientific visualization of volumetric data (e.g., Distributed 3D Gaussian Splatting). Key innovations include moving from spatial partitioning (which causes redundancy) to index-parity sharding (which balances load) [S2, unofficial], and introducing dynamic load balancing to handle the irregular visibility of Gaussians [S1][S2, unofficial]. The field also explores hardware-aware optimizations, such as the LS-Gaussian accelerator, to mitigate hardware stalls caused by workload imbalance [S1].
+The need for distributed training in 3D Gaussian Splatting arises from the quadratic growth of memory requirements with scene complexity and resolution. Early 3DGS implementations were limited to single-GPU capacities, typically handling scenes with up to ~10-20 million Gaussians depending on resolution [S1]. As applications expanded to city-scale reconstruction and scientific volume visualization, methods like [BlitzGS](15088-blitzgs.md) and [Distributed 3D Gaussian Splatting](16979-distributed-3d-gaussian-splatting.md) emerged to shard the model across multiple GPUs [S1]. Concurrently, systems like [Grendel](19770-grendel.md) and [Distributed Batched Rendering Loop](18495-distributed-batched-rendering-loop.md) focused on optimizing the communication overhead and load balancing inherent in distributed rasterization [S1]. The shift from spatial block partitioning to index-parity sharding was driven by the observation that spatial boundaries cause significant redundancy and imbalance in training workloads [S2, unofficial].
 
